\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Drift of the Space Charge During the Dark Period (Figure 4)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Bampaliaris Nikolaos
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
12-10-2026
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Summary
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Figure 4 of the Wang & Haiting paper shows 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
 near the rod tip before and after the space charge of the first corona streamer has drifted towards the plane. The figure is consistent with the cloud moving as a 
\series bold
rigid body
\series default
: curve 2 is curve 1 shifted by about 
\begin_inset Formula $0.07\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
. Reproducing this with the paper's drift law (Eq. 6) shows that the drift takes 
\begin_inset Formula $12.5\,\mu\text{s}$
\end_inset
 and that the 
\series bold
total
\series default
 field at the rod tip rises from 
\begin_inset Formula $-3.6$
\end_inset
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $+42.7\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
, which is the mechanism the paper proposes for the inception of the next streamer. A key point for the formulation is that only 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
 is a pure shift: the image fields must be computed by moving the 
\series bold
charge
\series default
 while the rod and the plane stay fixed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
What the Paper States
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The drift distance from 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{1}$
\end_inset
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{2}$
\end_inset
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{m}=\int_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}b\left[E_{ex}(Z_{c})+E_{I}(Z_{c})\right]dt$
\end_inset
 (Eq. 6), with 
\begin_inset Formula $b=1.36\times10^{-3}\,\text{cm}^{2}/(\text{kV}\cdot\mu\text{s})$
\end_inset
 the mobility of positive ions, and 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{c}$
\end_inset
 the point where 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}(Z_{c})=0$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Figure 4 shows 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
 before and after the drift; the drift time is not given.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
“A small drift toward the low voltage plane electrode can make a rapid increasing of the electric field in the surface of the rod tip, so as to that a new corona streamer can incept.”
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
“The drift of space charge also is one of the most important factors resulting in the occurring of dark period.”
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Reading Figure 4
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two observations show that the cloud is translated without deformation:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Eq. (6) defines a 
\series bold
single
\series default
 drift distance 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{m}$
\end_inset
 for the whole cloud, driven by the field at one point 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{c}$
\end_inset
. Every charge element is therefore moved by the same amount.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Curve 2 reaches a minimum of about 
\begin_inset Formula $-33\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
 at 
\begin_inset Formula $z\approx0.07\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
, the same value curve 1 has at the tip. This is where the apex of the drifted cone now sits. Between the rod and the drifted apex, curve 2 is the field seen behind the cloud: about 
\begin_inset Formula $-14\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
 at the tip, i.e. 
\begin_inset Formula $14/33=0.42$
\end_inset
 of the minimum. Our rigidly shifted cloud gives 
\begin_inset Formula $-11.65/-29.10=0.40$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Any change of 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{cs}$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset
 or 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset
 would change the shape or the size of the curve, not only its position.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Modelling Assumptions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The rigid drift rests on two 
\series bold
separate
\series default
 assumptions, which should be stated separately:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Constant charge.
\series default
 No ionization takes place during the dark period and recombination is negligible over microseconds, so 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{cs}$
\end_inset
, and with it 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
, stays constant.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Constant shape.
\series default
 Every ion moves with the same velocity 
\begin_inset Formula $b\left[E_{ex}+E_{I}\right]$
\end_inset
 evaluated at 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{c}$
\end_inset
. Constant charge alone does not imply this.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second assumption neglects three effects that would deform a real cloud: ions closer to the tip feel a stronger applied field and move faster than those further out, the cloud's own repulsion spreads it, and ions diffuse. The paper makes the same simplification.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The driving field contains no 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
: a charge distribution exerts no net force on itself, and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{c}$
\end_inset
 is by definition the point where 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}=0$
\end_inset
. Because 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
 moves rigidly with the cloud, its zero stays at 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{c}(t)=z_{c,0}+z_{\text{drift}}(t)$
\end_inset
. The paper's Eq. (6) includes only the rod image; we also include the plane image, which is negligible in an 
\begin_inset Formula $80\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
 gap.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
How Each Field Changes During the Drift
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The mapping 
\begin_inset Formula $z\rightarrow z_{\text{eff}}=z-z_{\text{drift}}$
\end_inset
 is exact only for 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
, because it depends only on the distance between charge and observer. The other fields involve the electrodes, which do not move (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
